\title{Finite Polyhedral Tropical Intersection Theory:\\Multiplicity Preservation, B\'ezout Counts, and Sharp Support Bounds}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 1, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained finite framework for weighted tropical intersections. A tropical variety is represented by a finite polyhedral complex equipped with codimension, nonnegative cell weights, a balancing condition, and degree. A zero-dimensional intersection is represented by a finite support with a natural-number local multiplicity function, and its intersection number is the sum of the supported multiplicities. Positive local multiplicities imply that support cardinality is bounded by total intersection number. Any bijective correspondence preserving support and local multiplicity preserves that number. The transverse plane model for curves of degrees $d$ and $e$, indexed by a Cartesian product of degree directions and carrying unit multiplicity, has intersection number exactly $de$ and attains the support bound. These conclusions transport to any classical finite intersection admitting such a correspondence. The arguments isolate the finite combinatorial core of tropical B\'ezout counting and identify the geometric input needed to pass between classical and tropical intersections.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Intersection theory counts where geometric objects meet, but its stable quantity is not generally the raw number of visible meeting points. Tangencies, collisions, and degenerations require local multiplicities. Several simple points may coalesce into one point of higher multiplicity: support shrinks while weighted total remains stable.

Tropical geometry expresses this principle in polyhedral language. Algebraic curves are replaced by balanced weighted complexes, and intersection problems become finite or locally finite combinatorial calculations. The passage to a tropical object can be sophisticated, but once support and multiplicity data have been matched, preservation of total intersection number is a finite reindexing theorem.

This paper studies that finite core. We do not assume that every geometric tropicalization automatically has the required properties. Instead, we formulate a precise multiplicity-preserving correspondence and derive its consequences. Geometric work constructs the bridge; finite intersection theory explains why the numerical invariant crosses it.

Our central example is the transverse intersection of plane tropical curves of degrees $d$ and $e$. We model its cells by pairs of degree directions. There are $de$ pairs, each carrying multiplicity one. The result is a finite tropical B\'ezout theorem and an explicit sharp cell bound. If a classical finite intersection corresponds to this model while preserving support and multiplicity, then its total is also $de$, and its support contains at most $de$ points.

\section{Finite polyhedral and weighted intersection data}

\subsection{Finite polyhedral tropical varieties}

A finite polyhedral complex is a finite collection of polyhedral cells closed under taking faces and whose intersections occur along common faces. The internal incidence representation is not needed below; only a finite indexed family on which weights can be assigned is required.

\begin{definition}[Finite polyhedral tropical variety]
A finite polyhedral tropical variety consists of a finite polyhedral complex $K$, a codimension $c\in\NN$, a cell-weight function $w$ with values in $\NN$, a balancing condition for the associated integer-weighted codimension-$c$ cycle, and a degree $d\in\NN$.
\end{definition}

The balancing condition is the local conservation law of tropical cycles. Around each relevant face, the weighted primitive normal directions sum to zero in the appropriate quotient lattice. Although the counting theorems below use only intersection supports and multiplicities, balancing distinguishes tropical cycles from arbitrary weighted cell collections. Degree is retained explicitly because the transverse plane count depends only on the two degrees.

\subsection{Finite weighted intersections}

\begin{definition}[Finite weighted intersection]
A finite weighted intersection is a triple $(X,S,m)$, where $X$ is a finite ambient point set, $S\subseteq X$ is the support, and $m:X\to\NN$ is a multiplicity function. Only values on $S$ contribute to the intersection number.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total intersection number]
The total weighted intersection number is
\[
I(S,m):=\sum_{p\in S}m(p).
\]
\end{definition}

The ambient set and support should not be conflated. The support records participating positions; the ambient set may include other candidate positions. In geometric intersection theory, transversality generally forces local multiplicity one. The finite model records this directly.

\section{Positive multiplicities and support cardinality}

\begin{theorem}[Positive-Multiplicity Support Bound]
Let $(X,S,m)$ be a finite weighted intersection. If $m(p)>0$ for every $p\in S$, then
\[
\card{S}\le I(S,m).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Because $m(p)$ is a positive natural number on $S$, one has $1\le m(p)$ for every $p\in S$. Summation gives
\[
\card{S}=\sum_{p\in S}1
\le\sum_{p\in S}m(p)=I(S,m).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unit multiplicities give equality]
If $m(p)=1$ for every $p\in S$, then $\card{S}=I(S,m)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Every summand defining the total equals one.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Equality characterization]
If all supported multiplicities are positive, then $\card{S}=I(S,m)$ if and only if $m(p)=1$ for every $p\in S$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The reverse implication is the preceding corollary. Conversely, if some $m(q)\ge2$, then that point contributes more than one while every other supported point contributes at least one. Hence $I(S,m)>\card{S}$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Thus every distinct point consumes at least one unit of the intersection total. Higher multiplicity consumes additional units without creating another location.

\section{Multiplicity-preserving correspondences}

\begin{definition}[Multiplicity-preserving tropicalization correspondence]
Let $(X_c,S_c,m_c)$ and $(X_t,S_t,m_t)$ be finite weighted intersections, interpreted as classical and tropical models. Such a correspondence is a bijection $\phi:X_c\to X_t$ satisfying
\[
p\in S_c\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\phi(p)\in S_t
\]
for every $p\in X_c$, and
\[
m_c(p)=m_t(\phi(p))
\]
for every $p\in S_c$.
\end{definition}

Support preservation makes the restriction of $\phi$ a bijection from $S_c$ to $S_t$. Multiplicity preservation makes this restriction an isomorphism of weighted finite sets.

\begin{theorem}[Tropicalization Invariance of Intersection Number]
If two finite weighted intersections admit a multiplicity-preserving tropicalization correspondence, then
\[
I(S_t,m_t)=I(S_c,m_c).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Reindex the sum over $S_c$ using the restricted bijection. Support preservation identifies the new index set with $S_t$, and multiplicity preservation identifies corresponding summands. Thus
\[
\sum_{p\in S_c}m_c(p)
=\sum_{p\in S_c}m_t(\phi(p))
=\sum_{q\in S_t}m_t(q).
\]
\end{proof}

The statement is conditional by design. Constructing $\phi$ may require valuation theory, a lifting theorem, or transversality. Once those geometric inputs are established, invariance is purely finite.

\begin{corollary}[Support cardinality preservation]
Under such a correspondence, $\card{S_c}=\card{S_t}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The restriction of $\phi$ is a bijection $S_c\to S_t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Transport of the positive-multiplicity bound]
If tropical supported multiplicities are positive, then classical supported multiplicities are positive and
\[
\card{S_c}\le I(S_c,m_c)=I(S_t,m_t).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Multiplicity equality transfers positivity. Apply the support bound and intersection-number invariance.
\end{proof}

\section{The transverse plane model}

For $d,e\in\NN$, set
\[
[d]=\{0,1,\ldots,d-1\},\qquad[e]=\{0,1,\ldots,e-1\}.
\]
If a degree is zero, its set is empty.

\begin{definition}[Transverse plane intersection model]
The transverse plane intersection of degrees $d$ and $e$ has ambient point set
\[
X_{d,e}=[d]\times[e],
\]
full support $S_{d,e}=X_{d,e}$, and constant multiplicity $m_{d,e}(i,j)=1$. The pair $(i,j)$ represents the cell obtained by pairing the $i$th degree direction of the first curve with the $j$th direction of the second.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Transverse Tropical B\'ezout Theorem]
For all $d,e\in\NN$,
\[
I(S_{d,e},m_{d,e})=de.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every member of $[d]\times[e]$ is supported and contributes one. Therefore
\[
I(S_{d,e},m_{d,e})
=\sum_{(i,j)\in[d]\times[e]}1
=\card{[d]\times[e]}.
\]
Finite Cartesian products satisfy
\[
\card{[d]\times[e]}=\card{[d]}\card{[e]}=de.
\]
The same calculation covers zero degrees, where the product set is empty.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp Tropical B\'ezout Cell Bound]
The transverse support satisfies $\card{S_{d,e}}\le de$, and equality holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The support is the full Cartesian product, whose cardinality is $de$. Equivalently, the positive-multiplicity bound becomes equality because all multiplicities are one.
\end{proof}

The weighted count and the number of distinct cells coincide here precisely because the model is transverse. For example, when $d=3$ and $e=4$, there are twelve pairs and total multiplicity twelve. A different support with multiplicities $(1,2,4,5)$ also has total twelve but only four points.

\section{Transport to classical intersections}

Suppose a classical finite intersection $(X_c,S_c,m_c)$ admits a multiplicity-preserving correspondence with the transverse plane model of degrees $d$ and $e$.

\begin{theorem}[Transported B\'ezout Theorem]
Under this hypothesis,
\[
I(S_c,m_c)=de.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Intersection-number invariance gives
\[
I(S_c,m_c)=I(S_{d,e},m_{d,e}),
\]
and the transverse tropical B\'ezout theorem evaluates the right side as $de$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transported Classical Support Bound]
Under the same hypothesis,
\[
\card{S_c}\le de.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Because $S_c\subseteq X_c$, one has $\card{S_c}\le\card{X_c}$. The ambient bijection identifies $X_c$ with $[d]\times[e]$. Hence
\[
\card{S_c}\le\card{X_c}
=\card{[d]\times[e]}=de.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Because support is preserved in both directions and the target has full support, the exact correspondence considered here actually forces every point of $X_c$ to be supported, so equality follows. The inequality formulation isolates the upper-bound mechanism and persists under weaker variants where ordinary support merely injects into tropical candidate cells.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[Classical unit multiplicity]
Every supported classical point has multiplicity one.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Every tropical target point has multiplicity one, and corresponding local multiplicities agree.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\begin{algorithm}[Weighted Intersection Audit]
Given supported multiplicities $(m_1,\ldots,m_n)$, verify that they are nonnegative integers; compute $n$ and $I=\sum_i m_i$; test whether every $m_i>0$; if so, certify $n\le I$; and report equality precisely when every $m_i=1$.
\end{algorithm}

This audit takes $O(n)$ time. For streamed data, the total and Boolean flags require $O(1)$ auxiliary space; storing all labels or values requires $O(n)$ space.

\begin{algorithm}[Transverse B\'ezout Cell Enumerator]
Given $d,e\in\NN$, loop over $0\le i<d$ and $0\le j<e$, outputting $(i,j)$ with multiplicity one. Return the cell list and total $de$.
\end{algorithm}

Enumeration takes $O(de)$ time and $O(de)$ output space, optimal when every cell is materialized. If only the total or bound is needed, direct multiplication uses a constant number of arithmetic operations, with bit complexity determined by the input sizes.

For explicitly labeled finite data, a correspondence audit verifies bijectivity, support equivalence, and multiplicity equality. Hash-based checking has expected linear time in the ambient cardinality; sorting-based checking takes $O(n\log n)$ time.

\section{Applications, scope, and limitations}

The support inequality models degeneration: unit intersections can coalesce, reducing support and increasing local multiplicity while leaving total weight unchanged. This explains why raw point counting is unstable and multiplicity is the conserved quantity.

The transverse construction converts degree data into Cartesian-product enumeration. More elaborate tropical intersections involve cones, lattice indices, and determinant multiplicities, but retain the same pattern: enumerate supported cells, assign nonnegative local weights, and sum. Any validated support-and-multiplicity correspondence can then transfer the result between representations.

The framework also separates responsibilities. The finite theorems require no hidden continuity argument, but they do not construct a geometric correspondence. A complete application must establish that a valuation or degeneration map is bijective on the relevant finite data, identifies support, and preserves local multiplicity.

Boundary cases are uniform. If $d=0$ or $e=0$, the transverse product is empty and the B\'ezout number is zero. Positivity on the empty support is vacuous, and all formulas remain valid.

The model is intentionally finite and transverse. It does not derive local multiplicity from determinants of primitive direction vectors, construct stable intersections by perturbation, prove realizability over a non-Archimedean field, or identify degree with Newton polygon data. It provides a precise finite target for those developments.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item \textbf{Mixed-area B\'ezout.} For balanced plane tropical curves with lattice Newton polygons $P$ and $Q$, develop the normalized mixed-area formula
\[
\operatorname{area}(P+Q)-\operatorname{area}(P)-\operatorname{area}(Q).
\]
\item \textbf{Perturbation invariance.} Show that a sufficiently small generic translation makes a finite balanced plane intersection transverse while preserving total local determinant multiplicity.
\item \textbf{Exact support criterion.} If the total is $de$ and supported multiplicities are positive, prove that support has size at most $de$, with equality exactly when every local multiplicity is one.
\item \textbf{Realizability correspondence.} For realizable plane tropical curves over a complete non-Archimedean field, establish a valuation-induced support- and multiplicity-preserving correspondence for transverse intersections.
\item \textbf{Higher-dimensional multidegrees.} For $n$ transverse tropical hypersurfaces in tropical projective $n$-space with degrees $d_1,\ldots,d_n$, develop the formula
\[
\prod_{i=1}^{n}d_i
\]
for total zero-dimensional stable-intersection multiplicity.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Finite tropical intersection theory reduces a stable geometric invariant to weighted combinatorics. Positive supported multiplicities bound distinct intersection points by total weight. A bijection preserving support and multiplicity preserves intersection number because it reindexes a finite sum. For transverse plane degrees $d$ and $e$, the cells form $[d]\times[e]$, all multiplicities are one, and both support cardinality and total equal $de$. A classical finite intersection linked to this model inherits the same B\'ezout count and support bound. Once the correct weighted correspondence is isolated, stable numerical content becomes a transparent product rule.

